\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The idea of TFHE's bootstrapping, a table lookup by rotation, drawn with $N=8$. {\fontfamily  {pzd}\fontencoding  {U}\fontseries  {m}\fontshape  {n}\selectfont  \char 172} The encrypted phase is rounded to one of $2N=16$ positions. {\fontfamily  {pzd}\fontencoding  {U}\fontseries  {m}\fontshape  {n}\selectfont  \char 173} The table of answers, stored as the coefficients of a polynomial $v$, is rotated by that position, which brings the right answer to the front (the entries that wrap around come back with a minus sign). {\fontfamily  {pzd}\fontencoding  {U}\fontseries  {m}\fontshape  {n}\selectfont  \char 174} The first entry is read. The hard part is step {\fontfamily  {pzd}\fontencoding  {U}\fontseries  {m}\fontshape  {n}\selectfont  \char 173}: the rotation depends on the secret key, so the server must rotate without knowing by how much.}}{6}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:plan}{{3}{6}{The idea of TFHE's bootstrapping, a table lookup by rotation, drawn with $N=8$. \ding {172} The encrypted phase is rounded to one of $2N=16$ positions. \ding {173} The table of answers, stored as the coefficients of a polynomial $v$, is rotated by that position, which brings the right answer to the front (the entries that wrap around come back with a minus sign). \ding {174} The first entry is read. The hard part is step \ding {173}: the rotation depends on the secret key, so the server must rotate without knowing by how much}{figure.3}{}}
