\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The CMux. (a) It chooses one of two TRLWE ciphertexts according to a bit $m$ that is itself encrypted, using one external product. (b) The case of Example~\ref {ex:cmux}: $d_0$ encrypts $\frac  18$ and $d_1$ encrypts $\frac  58$, so $d_1-d_0$ encrypts $\frac  12$. The external product adds $m\cdot \frac  12$ to the phase of $d_0$, which stays at $\frac  18$ when $m=0$ and moves half a turn, to $\frac  58$, when $m=1$. The server does the same work in both cases.}}{10}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cmux}{{5}{10}{The CMux. (a) It chooses one of two TRLWE ciphertexts according to a bit $m$ that is itself encrypted, using one external product. (b) The case of Example~\ref {ex:cmux}: $d_0$ encrypts $\frac 18$ and $d_1$ encrypts $\frac 58$, so $d_1-d_0$ encrypts $\frac 12$. The external product adds $m\cdot \frac 12$ to the phase of $d_0$, which stays at $\frac 18$ when $m=0$ and moves half a turn, to $\frac 58$, when $m=1$. The server does the same work in both cases}{figure.5}{}}
