\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{What the coefficients $v_j$ are.}{11}{paragraph*.17}\protected@file@percent }
\citation{chillotti2021pbs}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces What the coefficients $v_j$ of the test polynomial mean, for $N=4$. The circle is cut into $2N=8$ slices, numbered by the rounded phase $\bar  \varphi $ (outside), and each slice shows the value that the bootstrapping returns when the phase of its input is in that slice. Coefficient $v_j$ is the output for slice $j$ of the upper half (blue); the opposite slice $N+j$, in the lower half (orange), always returns $-v_j$, because $X^N=-1$. (a) The general rule. (b) The gate test polynomial: every $v_j$ is $\frac  18$, so the output is $+\frac  18$ on the upper half and $-\frac  18$ on the lower half. (c) A table that is not constant, $v_j=j/8$: on the upper half the bootstrapping returns the rounded phase itself.}}{12}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:testwheel}{{7}{12}{What the coefficients $v_j$ of the test polynomial mean, for $N=4$. The circle is cut into $2N=8$ slices, numbered by the rounded phase $\bar \varphi $ (outside), and each slice shows the value that the bootstrapping returns when the phase of its input is in that slice. Coefficient $v_j$ is the output for slice $j$ of the upper half (blue); the opposite slice $N+j$, in the lower half (orange), always returns $-v_j$, because $X^N=-1$. (a) The general rule. (b) The gate test polynomial: every $v_j$ is $\frac 18$, so the output is $+\frac 18$ on the upper half and $-\frac 18$ on the lower half. (c) A table that is not constant, $v_j=j/8$: on the upper half the bootstrapping returns the rounded phase itself}{figure.7}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Why rotating reads the right entry.}{12}{paragraph*.18}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Is the test polynomial encrypted?}{12}{paragraph*.19}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Programmable bootstrapping.}{12}{paragraph*.20}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The test polynomial as a lookup table, drawn for $N=4$. (a) The rounded phase $\bar  \varphi =\left \lfloor 2N\varphi  \right \rceil $ is one of $2N=8$ positions on the torus; positions $0$ to $N-1$ are the upper half and $N$ to $2N-1$ the lower half. (b) The coefficients of $X^{-\bar  \varphi }\cdot v$ for every $\bar  \varphi $: each row is the row above shifted to the left, and the coefficient that leaves on the left comes back on the right with its sign changed, because $X^N=-1$. The constant coefficient (framed) is $v_{\bar  \varphi }$ in the upper half and $-v_{\bar  \varphi -N}$ in the lower half. With every $v_j=\mu $, the output is $+\mu $ or $-\mu $ depending on the half of the circle where the phase is: the sign function used by every gate.}}{13}{figure.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:testpoly}{{8}{13}{The test polynomial as a lookup table, drawn for $N=4$. (a) The rounded phase $\bar \varphi =\round {2N\varphi }$ is one of $2N=8$ positions on the torus; positions $0$ to $N-1$ are the upper half and $N$ to $2N-1$ the lower half. (b) The coefficients of $X^{-\bar \varphi }\cdot v$ for every $\bar \varphi $: each row is the row above shifted to the left, and the coefficient that leaves on the left comes back on the right with its sign changed, because $X^N=-1$. The constant coefficient (framed) is $v_{\bar \varphi }$ in the upper half and $-v_{\bar \varphi -N}$ in the lower half. With every $v_j=\mu $, the output is $+\mu $ or $-\mu $ depending on the half of the circle where the phase is: the sign function used by every gate}{figure.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces The test polynomial is not encrypted. Blind rotation starts from the trivial TRLWE ciphertext $(0,X^{-\bar  b}v)$. Its mask is zero, so its phase is the public polynomial $X^{-\bar  b}v$ under any key (here $N=4$, $\bar  b=6$ and the gate test polynomial, as in Example~\ref {ex:blindrot}). The first CMux adds an external product with a ciphertext of the bootstrapping key, which is a real encryption under $s'$. After it, the accumulator has a random mask and some noise, and without the key one cannot see whether it was rotated (here by $X^{3}$).}}{13}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:trivial}{{9}{13}{The test polynomial is not encrypted. Blind rotation starts from the trivial TRLWE ciphertext $(0,X^{-\bar b}v)$. Its mask is zero, so its phase is the public polynomial $X^{-\bar b}v$ under any key (here $N=4$, $\bar b=6$ and the gate test polynomial, as in Example~\ref {ex:blindrot}). The first CMux adds an external product with a ciphertext of the bootstrapping key, which is a real encryption under $s'$. After it, the accumulator has a random mask and some noise, and without the key one cannot see whether it was rotated (here by $X^{3}$)}{figure.9}{}}
