\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Phases at the input of the bootstrapping, without noise. (a) NAND: every point is at distance $\frac  18$ from the closest boundary ($0$ or $\frac  12$); this distance is the noise margin. (b) XOR: with $c_x+c_y$, different inputs would fall on the boundary. After doubling, the two cases with equal inputs fall on the same point $\pm \frac  12$, and adding $\frac  14$ separates the two groups with a margin of $\frac  14$.}}{18}{figure.13}\protected@file@percent }
