\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20}{\ignorespaces One external product in our implementation. The digit polynomials of both components are transformed once and multiplied by the rows of the TRGSW ciphertext, which were transformed when the key was created. The products are added up in the frequency domain, so each external product needs only two inverse transforms. Blind rotation does one external product for each nonzero $\bar  a_i$, about $n=630$ per gate.}}{28}{figure.20}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:extprod}{{20}{28}{One external product in our implementation. The digit polynomials of both components are transformed once and multiplied by the rows of the TRGSW ciphertext, which were transformed when the key was created. The products are added up in the frequency domain, so each external product needs only two inverse transforms. Blind rotation does one external product for each nonzero $\bar a_i$, about $n=630$ per gate}{figure.20}{}}
